## The decision framework — tied to this project's own review-rigor tiers

This is not a parallel framework to this project's Tier 0/1/2 (or
equivalent) review-rigor policy — it's the same stakes-based reasoning,
extended to cover model choice for work generally, not just deep review.
If this table and that policy ever seem to disagree, that's a bug to fix
immediately, not a discretion call.

| Task | Right-sized choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Tier 0 work (docs, wording) | Session default does the work; self-merges if this project's policy allows it | Low stakes — no review escalation needed at all |
| Tier 0 supplementary check (e.g. "do these numbers agree across N docs") | Fast/cheap-model-appropriate, if dispatched as a separate pass at all | Mechanical, no judgment required — using a stronger model here is pure over-provisioning |
| Tier 1 work (features, app logic) | Session default builds it | Matches the task's real complexity |
| Tier 1 review (the required fresh pass) | A **fresh** invocation of the session default model | Decorrelation from the authoring context is the mechanism here, not model diversity |
| Tier 2 work (schema, compliance-adjacent, this doc/the agent-rules doc/CODEOWNERS) | Session default builds it | Same as Tier 1 — the escalation happens at review, not authorship |
| Tier 2 review | Fresh session-default pass, **PLUS** a deeper/multi-agent review mode (preferred, if available) **or**, if genuinely unavailable and confirmed, the Local Multi-pass fallback (fresh pass + one pass on a model different from whatever ran that fresh pass) | This is the one place real cross-model/cross-agent decorrelation earns its cost — see the agent rules doc's "Local multi-pass review" section |
| A review/task that naturally decomposes into independent angles | Dispatch multiple agents **in parallel** (only when the angles are genuinely independent — see "Parallel dispatch," below), each right-sized to *that angle's own* complexity | Parallelism is about independent angles running concurrently, not an excuse to escalate every angle to the strongest available model regardless of what that angle actually needs |
| Triage/dedupe of a large findings list before a human or a stronger pass looks at it | Fast/cheap-model-appropriate | Mechanical sorting/matching, not judgment |
| Verifying any finding against the real code before acting on it | **Never a fast/cheap-class model, full stop — an absolute floor, not relative to whatever the session default happens to be.** A capable/deep-reasoning-class model, whichever fits the underlying change's own tier. | This is an accountability-adjacent judgment call — never delegate it to a model class this playbook doesn't trust with judgment calls, and that trust boundary doesn't move just because a session's default happens to be a fast/cheap model |

## The relative-difference principle

Any rule that calls for "a different model" must be stated **relative to
whatever model actually ran the prior pass** — never hardcoded as a
fixed model name. If a fallback procedure says "switch to Model X" as a
literal, a session whose own default already *is* Model X would produce
two identical-model passes and a fabricated verification claim in the
risk summary if the label still says "different." When adding a new
model-selection rule anywhere in this project, state it as a property
("different from step 1's model," "stronger than the session default")
not a literal name, unless the rule is genuinely model-specific for a
reason that's written down.

## Parallel dispatch

Multiple agents may run concurrently when their tasks are **genuinely
independent** — no output of one is an input the others need first (the
same independence test any parallel tool dispatch should use generally).
Real, sanctioned uses:
- Splitting a large review into independent angles (correctness,
  security/data-handling, consistency-with-docs) so each runs at its own
  right-sized model instead of one pass trying to do everything at the
  strongest model available.
- Running a fast/cheap consistency check alongside a deeper pass, rather
  than making the deeper pass also do the mechanical checking.

Not a license to spin up agents by default — every dispatch still costs
real budget and real time to read the result. Parallelism helps when the
work genuinely splits; it doesn't help (and shouldn't be used) just
because it's available.

## Budget awareness is not optional

A deeper/multi-agent review mode is often its own billed service,
separate from the account's regular model usage — but "separate pool"
never means "free" or "unlimited." Both are real and finite. Before
reaching for a stronger-than-necessary model:
- Check whether the task's real tier actually calls for it (the table
  above).
- Don't escalate "just in case" — that's over-provisioning, a real
  failure mode per this doc's core principle, not a safe default.
- If a real budget constraint is already known, factor that into the
  choice rather than assuming headroom that hasn't been checked. A
  budget constraint discovered once is an instance-specific fact, not a
  standing rule that a review step can be skipped from now on — the
  human still gets asked fresh each time (see the agent rules doc's
  "Local multi-pass review" confirmation step).

## Hard boundaries — never regressed, no exceptions

- **A fast/cheap-class model never performs Tier 2 review, never makes
  an accountability-adjacent judgment call, and its output is never the
  final word on anything beyond mechanical/consistency-level work — this
  is an absolute floor, not relative to whatever the session default
  happens to be.** (A session whose own default is a fast/cheap model
  does not get to read this rule as satisfied by that default; it needs
  a genuinely capable-class pass for this specific step regardless.)
- **No model choice, however strong, ever substitutes for the human
  sign-off Tier 2 requires.** This playbook governs which AI does the
  *reviewing*; it has no authority over who does the *deciding*.
- **Any dispatch to a model other than the session default is announced
  to the human *before* it runs**, with a one-line reason — not just
  named after the fact in whatever summary/PR the work produces. Advance
  notice, not after-the-fact naming — the two are not equivalent.
- **This playbook must never contradict this project's own agent rules
  document.** They govern the same territory from two angles (review
  rigor vs. model choice) and must agree. A change to one that creates a
  conflict with the other is a bug, fixed on sight.
- **If this project has a manual-only model (see the table above), it is
  never selected automatically, by any agent, for any task — full stop,
  not a relative or situational call.** Every other row in the table is
  a *recommendation* an agent applies on its own judgment; a manual-only
  model is the one kind of exception, gated behind a human making the
  switch for that specific task. An agent that reasons "this task fits
  its description" still does not switch to it unprompted — it flags the
  fit and waits. This doesn't loosen as the model gets a real track
  record; only the *characterization* improves with use, not who may
  invoke it.

## Edge cases, resolved

- **The session's own default model changes.** Most rules here are
  stated relative to "the session default" or "whatever ran the prior
  pass," not a hardcoded name, and substitute correctly. **One
  deliberate exception:** the verification/judgment-call floor ("Hard
  boundaries," above) is an *absolute* floor, not a relative one — if
  the new default is itself a fast/cheap model, that floor does NOT
  lower with it. Check that row explicitly when the default changes;
  don't assume uniform substitution.
- **A task's tier is ambiguous.** Default to the higher tier, which
  means the more rigorous review-model choice too, not a weaker one.
- **A fast/cheap model's finding turns out to matter.** It still gets
  verified by a genuinely capable-class pass before anyone acts on it —
  speed is for surfacing candidates fast, not for being the last word.
- **Parallel agents disagree with each other.** Both findings get
  checked against the real code; the disagreement itself is informative
  (it often means the issue is genuinely ambiguous), not a
  tie-break-by-vote.
- **No model is characterized enough to trust for a given task.** Don't
  guess. Either use the best-characterized model that fits, or treat
  trying the uncharacterized option as its own small, explicit research
  step — verify before relying on it, not after.

